.lua
book.chapter(25, "Structured data and interchange")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Parsing is not schema validation"
.pp
The JSON module represents data with a Json ADT: JNull, JBool, JNum, JStr,
JArr, and JObj. Parse returns Result Json String. A successful parse proves
that the input belongs to the parser's accepted syntax, not that it contains
the fields and value ranges required by your application. Keep decoding into
a domain model as a separate step so syntax errors and schema errors retain
distinct explanations.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[parsed := JSON.parse "{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"scores\":[1,2]}"
Test.check "valid JSON" (Result.is_ok parsed)
value := Result.unwrap parsed
Test.equal "object field" (JSON.get "name" value) (Some (JStr "Ada"))
Test.equal "nested element"
  (JSON.get "scores" value |> Option.flat_map (JSON.at 1)) (Some (JNum 2.0))
Test.equal "compact output" (JSON.render (JArr [JBool true, JNull])) "[true,null]"]=],
    "Parse, inspect, and render a structured value")
.endlua
.pp
Object members are represented as lists of pairs. Get and at return None
when the requested operation does not apply or the requested member is
absent. Match directly on constructors when you need to distinguish a wrong
shape from a missing field. A decoder can then report "expected an array"
rather than collapsing every failure into "not found." Such distinctions
become valuable when configuration files contain several nested structures.
.sh 2 "Numbers and round trips"
.pp
JNum stores Float. Large integer tokens can therefore lose precision beyond
binary64's exact integer range. Encode identifiers as strings when exact
64-bit values must survive interchange. Render and pretty normalize output;
they do not preserve original whitespace, numeric spelling, or comments.
JSON.of_value is a convenience conversion, not a universal reversible
serialization of functions, abstract values, and foreign resources.
.pp
A stable protocol needs a versioned schema and explicit encoders. Decide
whether unknown fields are tolerated, how missing fields receive defaults,
and whether duplicate keys are rejected or normalized. Test decoding and
encoding separately as well as together. A round-trip test alone can allow
a matching bug in both directions to conceal an incorrect external format.
Include known byte-level fixtures from the protocol contract.
.sh 2 "Configuration formats share a data model"
.pp
TOML and YAML use the Json representation so common configuration consumers
can share validation code. Their in-tree parsers support practical subsets;
do not infer exhaustive standard conformance from the module names. Special
features such as aliases, tags, or source-preserving editing require explicit
support in the implementation and fixtures. TOML rendering returns Result
because not every Json value has a supported TOML representation.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "TOML value"
  (TOML.parse "answer = 42\n" |> Result.map (JSON.get "answer"))
  (Ok (Some (JNum 42.0)))
Test.equal "YAML value"
  (YAML.parse "name: demo\n" |> Result.map (JSON.get "name"))
  (Ok (Some (JStr "demo")))]=], "Different syntaxes can feed one validation layer")
.endlua
.pp
Normalized rendering is appropriate for generated configuration, but not
automatically for an editor that must retain user comments and formatting.
An editor generally needs a concrete syntax representation with trivia and
source spans. This is the same distinction encountered in language tools:
an abstract tree preserves meaning relevant to computation, while a
source-preserving tree retains additional information relevant to editing.
.sh 2 "S-expressions are a compact tree notation"
.pp
SExpr uses Atom String and SList containing child expressions. Parse accepts
multiple top-level expressions; parse_one requires one. Quoted atoms preserve
boundaries when text contains spaces or punctuation. This makes the format
useful for small intermediate languages, rewrite rules, and readable fixtures.
It does not assign semantics to a head atom: an expression beginning with add
means addition only because your application defines that interpretation.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "nested structure" (SExpr.parse_one "(a (b c))")
  (Ok (SList [Atom "a", SList [Atom "b", Atom "c"]]))
Test.check "reject malformed structure" (Result.is_err (SExpr.parse_one "(a"))]=],
    "Tree notation before semantic interpretation")
.endlua
.pp
ASTBuilder converts between its generic tree representation and S-expressions,
with validation of the expected shape. Handle the conversion Result rather
than assuming every syntactically valid S-expression is a valid AST. A
compiler can use several successive validation boundaries: bytes to
S-expression, S-expression to generic syntax, and generic syntax to a domain
ADT. Each boundary establishes a stronger invariant for subsequent passes.
.sh 2 "Hashes and URI encodings operate on bytes"
.pp
Hash offers FNV-1a, DJB2, CRC32, SHA-256, and helpers for combining or displaying
integer hashes. Choose the algorithm according to the purpose: a checksum,
a hash-table key, and a content digest have different requirements. Hash.value
uses displayed values and should not become a schema-independent persistent
identifier. Canonicalize the intended byte representation before hashing when
reproducible content identity matters.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "CRC fixture" (Hash.crc32 "123456789") 3421780262
Test.equal "FNV fixture" (Hash.hex (Hash.fnv1a "hello")) "a430d84680aabd0b"
Test.equal "component encoding" (URI.encode "a b/") "a%20b%2F"
Test.equal "invalid escape" (URI.decode "%zz") None
Test.equal "repeated query keys" (URI.query_params "x=a+b&x=two")
  [("x", "a b"), ("x", "two")]]=], "Byte-oriented utility contracts")
.endlua
.pp
URI parsing returns a structured record through Option. Query decoding treats
plus as space and preserves repeated keys as pairs. Component encoding is
different from encoding an entire URI with its structural separators. The
compact URI representation does not preserve every lexical distinction and
is not a complete browser URL engine. Apply the application's own rules
before treating decoded paths as filesystem locations or accepted origins.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read the JSON, TOML, YAML, SExpr, Hash, and URI host modules and their tests.
Write a configuration decoder that requires a string name and a nonnegative
integer count, distinguishing absent fields from wrong types. Decide how it
handles fractional JNum values. Then define a canonical serialized form for
that validated configuration and hash it, testing that irrelevant input
whitespace changes do not alter the resulting content identity.
